<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation by default. Test projects opt out by
    setting <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    in their csproj. (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because
    the SDK locks in its decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was previously gated on
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild, a property nothing in this repository sets.
    The SDK does not infer it from a CI environment: measured on 10.0.400 it
    evaluates empty even with CI and GITHUB_ACTIONS set, and only an explicit
    -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true gives it a value. The one workflow here,
    publish.yml, restores without passing it. So the gate never fired in this
    repository at all, including on the path that builds and publishes the
    package — a package could be produced from a graph the committed lock did
    not describe. That path is now enforced too, which is the intended effect.

    A file whose promise depends on a switch set somewhere else cannot state
    its own promise. Removing the condition makes the promise this file's own,
    which is the reason to prefer it over giving the workflow the flag.

    The failure it prevents is not hypothetical. A committed lock file
    elsewhere in this project recorded a dependency at preview1 against a
    preview2 producer for weeks, through four arcs, and every local build
    passed: a local restore *updates* a lock rather than enforcing it, so the
    drift was invisible to every gate. Locked mode is what turns that into a
    failed restore.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now needs a restore told to
    re-evaluate the lock — `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`,
    which is what the CLI's force-evaluate switch sets — rather than a plain
    restore. One command, once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole
    purpose is that it does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here
    as a property because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
